1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,005
pitzDaily, run with the k-epsilon model. Its controlDict adds a probe and a line sampled across the channel.

2
00:00:10,005 --> 00:00:18,505
Plot Results lists everything the run wrote: the sampled line, the probes, and the residuals in the solver log.

3
00:00:18,505 --> 00:00:29,678
Tick a column and it is plotted. Residuals get a log scale.

4
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:34,308
A sampled line is plotted at its latest time: the velocity across the channel, behind the step.

5
00:00:34,308 --> 00:00:44,405
The Time slider steps it through its output times, the axes held still.

6
00:00:44,405 --> 00:00:53,405
Add Case brings in a second run: the same case with the k-omega SST model.

7
00:00:53,405 --> 00:01:06,651
Plot This Column in Every Case ticks it in both. The legend names only what tells the lines apart: the case and its sample time.

8
00:01:06,651 --> 00:01:10,305
Title and axis labels go below the plot, beside ranges, tick spacing and log scales.

9
00:01:10,305 --> 00:01:18,733
Drag the legend where you want it. Saved images and scripts put it there too.

10
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:32,505
Or pick a place from the list, outside the plot included.

11
00:01:32,505 --> 00:01:38,253
Export Script writes a matplotlib or gnuplot script that draws this plot again, reading the case files each time it runs.

12
00:01:38,253 --> 00:01:43,785
Export Data saves the plotted lines themselves as a CSV with headings, and a note of where each came from.

13
00:01:43,785 --> 00:01:50,705
Hover over a data set to see the files it was read from.

14
00:01:50,705 --> 00:01:54,705
Right-click it to open the file itself, read-only: here, one velocity vector per probe per iteration.
